---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';

// Every claim on this page is one that `docs/privacy-audit.md` in the Rhythm repository
// already evidences — §2 for the App Store questionnaire, §3 for the permissions and
// declarations, §5 for the storage inventory. If the app changes, that file changes
// first and this page follows it. Do not add a claim this page cannot point at.
//
// Two things this page deliberately does NOT say, because they are not true of v1:
//   · iCloud sync. FR-1004 is post-v1 and CloudKit is not linked. `docs/store-listing.md`
//     §6 refuses the claim outright. What IS true is the system's own device backup,
//     which the "What leaves your device" section states instead.
//   · Anything about how collected data is handled. Nothing is collected, and describing
//     handling would imply a channel that does not exist.
const lastUpdated = '27 August 2026';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Rhythm — Privacy policy"
  section="rhythm"
  description="Rhythm collects no data. It has no account, no analytics, and no network code at all."
>
  <main>
    <header>
      <a class="back" href="/rhythm/">
        <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true">
          <line x1="19" y1="12" x2="5" y2="12"></line>
          <polyline points="11 18 5 12 11 6"></polyline>
        </svg>
        <span>Rhythm</span>
      </a>
      <h1>Privacy policy</h1>
      <span class="date">Last updated {lastUpdated}</span>
    </header>

    <p class="lede">Rhythm collects no data.</p>

    <p>
      There is no account, no analytics, no tracking, and no code in it from anyone else. There is
      no network code in it at all — the app links no networking framework — so it makes no
      requests, sends nothing anywhere, and works with the network off. Its App Store privacy label
      reads <em>Data Not Collected</em>. That is not a promise about how data is handled. There is
      no channel for it to travel down.
    </p>

    <section>
      <h2>What Rhythm stores, and where</h2>
      <p>
        Everything you make in Rhythm stays on your device, in the app's own storage: your presets
        and folders, your songs, setlists and practice routines, your practice history, your
        settings, and any sound you import. Nobody else can see it, and that includes me. Deleting
        the app removes all of it.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>What leaves your device</h2>
      <p>
        Only what you send yourself. <em>Settings &rsaquo; Data &rsaquo; Back up</em> writes one
        file holding your library, and where it goes next is your decision, made in the iOS share
        sheet. Rhythm has no other way out.
      </p>
      <p>
        If you back up your iPhone or iPad, iOS includes Rhythm's storage in that backup, along
        with every other app's. That backup is made by the system, under your own Apple Account,
        and Rhythm neither performs it nor reads it.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Permissions</h2>
      <p>
        Rhythm asks for none, at launch or afterwards. It uses no camera, no microphone, no
        location, no contacts, no photo library and no notifications, and it declares no permission
        strings — which is why no prompt appears when you open it.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Diagnostics</h2>
      <p>
        While the click is running, Rhythm writes a few timing markers into your device's own
        system log, which is where iOS keeps that kind of thing. They can be read on the device, by
        whoever is holding it, and they are readable nowhere else. Nothing is uploaded, aggregated
        or sent to me.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>The link inside the app</h2>
      <p>
        The App Store requires Rhythm to carry a link to this page, and it does, on the About
        screen. Tapping it hands the address to your browser; Rhythm does not fetch the page,
        embeds no web view, and has no way to learn whether you opened it.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Children</h2>
      <p>Rhythm is rated 4+. It collects nothing from anyone, of any age.</p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Changes</h2>
      <p>
        If this policy changes, the new version will be at this address, with the date it changed.
        There is no mailing list to announce it on, because there is no list.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Contact</h2>
      <p>Write to <a href="mailto:geoff@zorched.net">geoff@zorched.net</a>.</p>
    </section>
  </main>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  /* Same scale as /rhythm/ — docs/wordmark-and-type.md §3. */
  main {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 36px;
    max-width: 640px;
    padding: clamp(48px, 7vw, 72px) 0 clamp(40px, 6vw, 64px);
    font-size: 17px;
    font-weight: 400;
    line-height: 1.6;
  }

  header {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 18px;
    margin-bottom: 8px;
  }

  .back {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 10px;
    align-self: flex-start;
    font-family: var(--mono);
    font-size: 11px;
    letter-spacing: 0.16em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
  }

  .back svg {
    width: 14px;
    height: 14px;
    display: block;
    flex-shrink: 0;
  }

  h1 {
    margin: 0;
    font-size: clamp(34px, 5vw, 44px);
    font-weight: 500;
    letter-spacing: -0.03em;
    line-height: 1.05;
  }

  .date {
    font-family: var(--mono);
    font-size: 11px;
    letter-spacing: 0.16em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--faint);
  }

  h2 {
    margin: 0;
    font-size: 22px;
    font-weight: 500;
    letter-spacing: -0.02em;
    line-height: 1.35;
  }

  section {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 12px;
  }

  p {
    margin: 0;
  }

  .lede {
    font-size: 22px;
    line-height: 1.5;
    color: var(--bright);
  }

  /* The app spells its own screens this way; italics would read as emphasis rather
     than as a name, so these are set in the brighter text colour instead. */
  em {
    font-style: normal;
    color: var(--soft);
  }
</style>
